# TODO - Uninterrupted autonomous quiz authoring assurance

## Objective

Make the admitted end-to-end AI-to-quiz flow genuinely autonomous in routine
conditions, with safe recovery from provider delay and observable progress.

## Acceptance

Prove a registered-tool journey from a teacher request through drafted content,
explicit admitted media intake, creation or supported edits, and independently
fresh Blooket verification, without a controller using Blooket Save or other
routine editing controls. The account can require a human for actual sign-in,
CAPTCHA, MFA, a real provider restriction or renewed permission; these stops
must never silently retry an uncertain mutation.

Demonstrate cancellation, read-only recovery and target identity preservation
when the provider is delayed. Distinguish portable regressions from actual
Chrome for Testing evidence and do not overwrite older publication journals.

## Evidence

The completed `blooket-12` milestone has dated positive authenticated local
MCP/CLI creation, image/typing content and text replacement verification.
This follow-up narrows to uninterrupted autonomous behavior and current
Chrome for Testing behavior after those completed functional checkpoints.
It is a new task, not reopening or rewriting the completed milestone.

On 2026-10-21, an adversarial red-to-green Studio regression found that a
read-only reconciler could return a different set, revision or operation count
while still reporting `reconciliation-required`, `wait` or even `ready`.
Another final verification stop could show a foreign target without proving
success.

Studio now preserves the exact immutable checkpoint for every
inconclusive read response and never presents `ready` as recovery from an
uncertain Save. Legitimate unchanged rate-limit waits remain admitted.
This is portable safety evidence, not another remote mutation.

A separate red-to-green regression found that the Studio command sender could
mutate a reused `draftId` payload during the first status call, causing later
reconciliation or verification to target an unrelated draft. Each canonical
command now receives a fresh argument object. Tests retain the proper draft
identity through both the completed-checkpoint verification path and the
read-only recovery path, without performing provider writes.

The authenticated Chrome for Testing session separately returned a canonical
`my-sets` state and two consecutive successful read-only question collections
for the existing private quiz, each with one multiple-choice and one typing
question. This supports current read interoperability, not proof of a new
uninterrupted AI-authored publication.

The same checkpoint discipline now applies to nonadvancing results returned
from an ordinary publication step. A synthetic `ready`, `recovered` or
foreign/rolled-back `wait` cannot become a usable UI resume point.
Only genuine provider waits, sign-in requirements or human challenges
may pause, with an unchanged frozen journal (or no initial snapshot).
This blocks duplicate-save confusion without adding busy retries.

Studio now presents a direct explicit Resume action after read-only recovery
of an incomplete plan instead of requiring an additional status Refresh.
This does not automatically send Save: the teacher must click Resume, and
the canonical orchestrator re-reads the immutable snapshot first.
An inconclusive read never exposes that action. A late recovered reply after
Stop also cannot re-enable a publication button; a separate fresh Refresh
is needed if the teacher later elects to continue.

A concurrent full validation exposed an over-tight synthetic fixture budget:
`capabilities.inspect-text` may own a bounded eight-second detached tab
inspection, while the worker test required a reply within three seconds.
The unchanged read completed when the worker test ran alone (six of six
passed). The fixture now allows twelve seconds for this explicit capability
operation, without extending product read deadlines or changing write safety.

A red-to-green 2026-10-21 Studio regression found that nested or wrongly
typed provider stop `state`, `reason` and `verifiedAt` fields could enter a
frozen progress snapshot without failing validation before its next write.
The presentation boundary now requires string diagnostic values and refuses
object or numeric forms. The fix does not interpret external provider HTML or
infer any remote receipt.

On 2026-10-21, portable red-to-green tests added a bounded, explicitly
opted-in five-second cooldown for a **read-only** `reconcile` or `verify`
after a rate-limited reply. The Studio uses an abortable timer so Stop exits
immediately. Only one retry is possible, a second limit returns the stop,
and a human challenge or other wait does not trigger a retry.

Crucially, no uncertain `publication.step` is ever repeated: a recovered Save
must first be proved by the canonical journal's fresh provider reads. This is
not a claim of currently available authenticated Chrome verification.

The current authenticated development browser was unavailable to the product
bridge (HTTP bootstrap reported disconnected; the canonical session read
returned `blooket-browser-unavailable`, and the existing DevTools port refused
connection). A red-to-green UI regression now distinguishes this actionable
connection stop from ordinary remote verification failures, with ES/EN
instructions to reuse the existing profile, retain journals and never repeat
an uncertain Save. No browser reload, account write or replacement login was
performed in this session.

An additional Studio control-lifetime test exercised the actual Quizzes
closure using its abort signal while the bounded read-only cooldown was
pending. Stop immediately resolved the timer, removed the active Stop control
and exposed only the next safe status refresh. No write or additional sender
invocation was introduced during cancellation.

On 2026-10-21, a red-to-green Stop regression exercised delayed replies
from each of status, one in-flight publication.step, read-only reconciliation
and final verification. Previously Stop could return `paused` but still
report a late `advanced`, `recovered` or `verified` state to UI listeners.
After the correction, no late response is reported or used for a subsequent
command once the explicit teacher intent is canceled. Stop does not abort an
already submitted remote Save or promise it was undone: any ambiguity stays
in the canonical journal for a separately authorized read-only recovery.

A second 2026-10-21 red-to-green cancellation regression exercised the real
Studio Quizzes closure. Even when the underlying orchestrator was stopped,
late resolved `verified` or `recovered` responses could repaint the UI after
Stop, falsely implying active success or progress. The visual controller now
ignores progress callbacks after its abort signal and always displays Paused
for any late successful command response. Subsequent writes remain gated by
a new explicit teacher click and canonical status refresh.

A separate 2026-10-21 Studio red-to-green regression found that its direct
read-only Reconcile button could expose Resume or Verify after an incomplete
`recovered` response with no draft identity or matching frozen checkpoint.
The action is now admitted only after strict own-field decoding and exact
revision, remote set, operation count and draft identity checks. The next
explicit teacher click still rereads canonical status before any mutation.
Invalid or inconclusive recovery offers only a fresh status Refresh.

A third 2026-10-21 red-to-green Studio regression found that the standalone
Verify action could display `publicationVerified` after a malformed,
cross-draft or cross-set reply. It now compares the complete frozen draft,
revision, set and operation count, and requires the server's verified-at field
for a successful claim. A genuinely inconclusive rate-limited read still
presents its own wait status; no additional Save is issued.

A fourth red-to-green 2026-10-21 Studio regression demonstrated that merely
refreshing local publication status could display `publicationVerified` from
a forged `published: true` response, despite no fresh Blooket read. Quizzes
now decodes its local status as a strict not-started, ready, writes-complete
or reconciliation-required checkpoint with `published: false`, matching the
registered canonical command. Fresh Verify alone may show completion.

A fifth 2026-10-21 red-to-green Studio regression exercised failed initial and
subsequent publication status requests. Initially the page had no way to retry;
a later transient error left an obsolete Publish control visible. The Quizzes
view now removes all stale actions after a missing or invalid status and
provides only the read-only Refresh button. A newly validated journal can
restore an explicit action; the failure itself never schedules a Save.

A 2026-10-21 red-to-green orchestration regression found that automated
`publication.verify` could report success without the canonical
`verifiedAt` evidence, although standalone Verify already required that
field. All automated positive results now require a nonblank verification
timestamp in addition to an exact immutable journal and target. Negative
verification stops remain read-only and do not imply a new Save.

A second 2026-10-21 red-to-green test found that a synchronous teacher Stop
from a rate-limit progress callback still allowed the UI to start its next
read-only cooldown timer. The orchestrator now checks cancellation immediately
after the callback, so no timer or provider retry is scheduled under a revoked
intent. Existing interrupted Save receipts remain journaled and un-replayed.

The registered MCP integration now drives a single uninterrupted Studio-style
publication intent across actual MCP tool descriptors, CLI subprocesses,
localhost service, journaled broker and a sanitized synthetic extension.
Its two sequential provider mutations are requested by the orchestrator,
not manually replayed by the test, and the final receipt requires verifiedAt.
This is a real multi-process integration with a fake Blooket provider, not
a fresh authenticated provider Save or recipient-ChatGPT observation.

The MCP tool annotation contract was audited against the 2026-03-16 MCP
maintainer guidance. The `reconcile` tool observes only the provider but may
update an existing local checkpoint; it is therefore not annotated as pure
read-only. It is now explicitly non-destructive and idempotent, unlike the
non-idempotent `step`, `append` and `replace` mutations. An integration test
checks every relevant annotation to avoid unnecessary AI-client warnings or
unsafe claims about remote Save replay.

The multi-process MCP integration was extended to lose exactly one browser
acknowledgement **after** its simulated Add Question Save persisted. The
canonical append returns an uncertain journal; the uninterrupted owner then
uses status, read-only reconcile and fresh verify without dispatching a
second questions.create. The test asserts the final two-question remote
collection and exact mutation sequence. No authenticated Blooket data was
written to produce this evidence.

The autonomous and manual Verify presentation paths now share strict
validation of the canonical server's UTC ISO timestamp. A nonempty arbitrary
label or an impossible calendar date cannot serve as a remote verification
receipt; neither check trusts a timestamp alone without the exact frozen
journal identity. The prior synthetic tests passed malformed timestamps;
current red-to-green tests reject them.

A later red-to-green progress regression rejected contradictory phase metadata:
ordinary `ready`, `advanced` or `verified` receipts cannot carry a provider
challenge `state`, a `write-not-confirmed` reconciliation reason or an
out-of-phase verification timestamp. Native stop metadata is accepted only
with its corresponding bounded stop phase. This prevents mislabeling an
uncertain read as another authorized mutation or as verified success.
